 "title": "Actor Tips for Sounding Natural on Camera",
 "topic": "content creation",
 "summary": "The creator disagrees with typical advice to look below the lens and use hand gestures to sound natural on camera. Drawing on her professional acting experience in commercials, she suggests imagining someone you love is down the lens and directing your attention outward to help you deliver your message naturally.",
 "key_points": [
  "The creator disagrees with looking just below the center of the lens for eye contact.",
  "The creator disagrees with using hand gestures to try to get out of your head, claiming it adds a challenge and feels like multitasking while delivering a message.",
  "Professional acting training (for brands like Honda, Google, Walmart, Best Buy) teaches actors to look \"down the barrel\" of the lens.",
  "Imagine putting someone you love down into the lens and talking through the lens to them.",
  "Instead of giving your hands something to do, put your attention somewhere else by thinking about the person you are helping.",
  "Think about what you want the audience to take away from the video and how you want to make them feel."
 ],
 "content": "Instagram reel/post by Sonya Wills | Actor Tips → Creators. Text / caption: How to sound natural on camera? Careful from whom you take advice! Here’s what professional actors do to sound believable on camera. Use the tips to sound more like YOU! I just watched a video about how to sound natural on camera and I just disagree. The first point was about eye contact. She suggests looking just below the center of the lens to look more natural. Second, she says do something with your hands so you can get out of your head and out of your way. As a trained actor, may I suggest doing this instead? I've done TV commercials for Honda and Google and Walmart and Best Buy and we were taught look down the barrel. It's like you put someone you love down into that lens and then you talk through the lens to them. Watch how that looks. Here's just looking: Hi, I'm Sonya. And now here's someone I love down the lens. Hi, I'm Sonya. Second, she says do something with your hands. And I disagree with this one because I've worked with a lot of creators and I know that's actually adding a challenge into the mix. If you feel like folding your laundry while you deliver your message, feels like patting your head and rubbing your tummy at the same time, you're not alone. It is important to stop listening to yourself, but instead of giving your hands something to do, why don't you give your attention somewhere to go? When you film, think about the person that you're helping, think about the thing that you want them to take away from this video, how you want to make them feel. Whoa, my light is moving. That's better. This is all that we do inside of the Camera Smart Creator community, so if getting better on camera is your goal right now, comment \"READY\" and I'll send you more information. On screen text: Pro Actor v Content Creator (Where to look when recording)",
